<article class="env-lemma" id="00H7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00H7">Lemma <span data-tag="00H7">10.38.6</span>.</a> Let $R \subset S$ be an inclusion of domains. Assume $R$ is normal. Let $g \in S$ be integral over $R$. Then the minimal polynomial of $g$ has coefficients in $R$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $P = x^ m + b_{m-1} x^{m-1} + \ldots + b_0$ be a polynomial with coefficients in $R$ such that $P(g) = 0$. Let $Q = x^ n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \ldots + a_0$ be the minimal polynomial for $g$ over the fraction field $K$ of $R$. Then $Q$ divides $P$ in $K[x]$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00H6" data-tag="00H6">10.38.5</a> we see the $a_ i$ are integral over $R$. Since $R$ is normal this means they are in $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>